WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.551
Your agents spend most of the day waiting.

00:00:02.551 --> 00:00:05.114
So why is every one of them still holding memory?

00:00:09.165 --> 00:00:10.222
Hi, I'm Brielle!

00:00:10.222 --> 00:00:15.155
Agent Substrate is a Kubernetes runtime that turns a waiting agent into a snapshot.

00:00:15.155 --> 00:00:18.325
Let's see how, and what its density claim measures.

00:00:20.008 --> 00:00:20.733
Chapter one.

00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:22.183
An agent mostly waits.

00:00:26.275 --> 00:00:28.072
Follow one turn of a coding agent.

00:00:29.142 --> 00:00:32.354
It sends the conversation to a model and waits for tokens.

00:00:32.774 --> 00:00:35.239
It runs a tool, a search or a test.

00:00:35.239 --> 00:00:36.882
Only this part works the sandbox.

00:00:37.301 --> 00:00:40.158
Then it waits for a person: minutes, hours, or a day.

00:00:41.525 --> 00:00:43.425
Kubernetes gives you two bad options.

00:00:43.425 --> 00:00:46.593
Keep a pod per session and pay for idle memory.

00:00:46.593 --> 00:00:51.027
Or delete pods between turns, and pay a cold start and the lost state.

00:00:51.447 --> 00:00:53.110
Substrate takes a third way.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:56.768
A waiting agent becomes a snapshot, and comes back on whichever

00:00:56.768 --> 00:00:58.098
warm worker is free.

00:00:59.842 --> 00:01:00.592
Chapter two.

00:01:00.592 --> 00:01:02.091
Actors on warm workers.

00:01:06.058 --> 00:01:07.095
Here's the idea.

00:01:07.095 --> 00:01:10.553
Freeze an idle agent, memory and files, into a snapshot.

00:01:10.553 --> 00:01:14.702
When the next request arrives, restore it onto any free warm worker.

00:01:16.742 --> 00:01:19.494
Picture a fleet of actors, each a stateful agent.

00:01:19.494 --> 00:01:23.164
While they wait, each is just a snapshot in storage, and the

00:01:23.164 --> 00:01:24.999
worker pods sit warm and empty.

00:01:25.419 --> 00:01:26.143
Requests arrive.

00:01:26.143 --> 00:01:29.766
Three actors are restored, memory and all, into free slots.

00:01:30.186 --> 00:01:31.490
One finishes its turn.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:35.076
A suspend call checkpoints it back to storage, freeing its slot.

00:01:35.496 --> 00:01:37.471
A request comes for a different actor.

00:01:37.471 --> 00:01:39.165
It wakes into the freed slot.

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:40.576
Any free worker will do.

00:01:42.175 --> 00:01:45.756
A request names its actor in a header and reaches the router:

00:01:45.756 --> 00:01:47.846
Envoy, with a small processor in front.

00:01:48.266 --> 00:01:52.563
If the actor is suspended, the router asks the control plane to resume it.

00:01:52.563 --> 00:01:55.940
Under a lease, the scheduler samples two workers with room and

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:57.167
takes the less loaded.

00:01:57.587 --> 00:02:01.051
On that node, a supervisor restores the snapshot into a sandbox, from

00:02:01.051 --> 00:02:04.227
a local copy if it has one, else from the bucket.

00:02:04.647 --> 00:02:07.952
Then the router tunnels the request in, over mutual TLS.

00:02:09.717 --> 00:02:13.801
The project draws the same path, numbered: route, resume, restore from

00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:15.287
the snapshot bucket, deliver.

00:02:15.707 --> 00:02:19.301
And on the way out, every tool call goes through the egress

00:02:19.301 --> 00:02:20.798
gateway, which holds the credentials.

00:02:22.717 --> 00:02:25.088
Each template is booted once and checkpointed.

00:02:25.088 --> 00:02:29.492
New actors start from that golden snapshot, so none pays a cold boot.

00:02:29.912 --> 00:02:31.203
A request resumes it.

00:02:31.203 --> 00:02:33.785
A cold start has become a memory restore.

00:02:34.205 --> 00:02:37.174
While it runs, it holds a slot at its full memory limit.

00:02:37.594 --> 00:02:41.117
When your harness calls suspend, the checkpoint lands on the node's disk

00:02:41.117 --> 00:02:42.291
and the call returns.

00:02:42.711 --> 00:02:46.325
The upload follows five minutes later, and the local copy stays for

00:02:46.325 --> 00:02:48.735
thirty, so a quick wake skips the download.

00:02:50.508 --> 00:02:54.252
The project's own demo: three Claude Code agents sharing two worker pods.

00:02:54.252 --> 00:02:56.124
The third waits for a pod.

00:02:57.883 --> 00:02:58.490
Chapter three.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:00.009
Where ten x comes from.

00:03:04.608 --> 00:03:07.509
Take a fleet of N agents, each with a memory limit M.

00:03:07.929 --> 00:03:11.110
One sandbox per agent holds N times M, all the time.

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:15.633
Multiplexed, only the S running actors hold a slot, each at its

00:03:15.633 --> 00:03:17.685
limit plus a small runtime overhead.

00:03:18.105 --> 00:03:21.333
If agents run a tenth of the time and never collide, S

00:03:21.333 --> 00:03:24.023
is a tenth of N, and the ratio is ten.

00:03:24.023 --> 00:03:25.638
The multiple is the duty cycle.

00:03:27.733 --> 00:03:30.224
Perfect staggering gives ten x at any size.

00:03:30.224 --> 00:03:33.338
But you size a pool for busy moments, not averages.

00:03:33.758 --> 00:03:37.338
Sized for the ninety-ninth percentile, two hundred and fifty agents get

00:03:37.338 --> 00:03:38.640
six point eight x.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.331
Only at a million agents does it reach nine point nine.

00:03:42.331 --> 00:03:45.008
That is why millions and ten x travel together.

00:03:46.592 --> 00:03:47.243
Chapter four.

00:03:47.243 --> 00:03:48.870
What zero trust means here.

00:03:53.317 --> 00:03:55.657
Zero trust has a definite shape in this code.

00:03:56.077 --> 00:03:59.109
Every running actor gets its own short-lived certificate.

00:03:59.529 --> 00:04:01.912
All outbound traffic goes through one gateway.

00:04:01.912 --> 00:04:04.975
No rule, or a failed lookup, means no traffic.

00:04:05.395 --> 00:04:09.090
The agent sends a placeholder header, and the gateway swaps in the

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:10.630
real key outside the sandbox.

00:04:12.025 --> 00:04:14.188
The code also shows what is not there yet.

00:04:14.614 --> 00:04:17.512
Nothing in this commit decides an agent is idle.

00:04:17.512 --> 00:04:18.800
Your harness calls suspend.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.359
The benchmark harness is in the repository.

00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:23.803
The run behind the ten x is not.

00:04:24.223 --> 00:04:28.571
And the Claude Code demo puts its API key in plain environment variables.

00:04:29.642 --> 00:04:33.564
Idle agents become snapshots, so you pay only for the ones running.

00:04:33.564 --> 00:04:35.524
The density is your duty cycle.

00:04:35.524 --> 00:04:36.505
Measure it first.

00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:38.740
To recap.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:42.107
Idle actors live as snapshots and wake on warm workers.

00:04:42.107 --> 00:04:44.800
Density tends to one over their running share.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.483
Keep keys at the gateway.

00:04:46.903 --> 00:04:48.986
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:48.986 --> 00:04:49.581
I'm Brielle.

00:04:49.581 --> 00:04:49.879
Bye!
